// A comprehensive tour of the `automaton` package — finite-state machines and
// state-transition diagrams drawn as a thin layer of automata-theory conventions
// over the `diagram` package.
//
// Render with:  texish scripts/automaton-demo.script
//
// `document` gives us a title block, numbered section headings and paragraph
// indentation; `automaton` gives us the state and transition vocabulary.

\use{document}
\use{automaton}

\def headline {\italic{The automaton package} \hfil \the\pageno}
\set raggedbottom {1}

// A small helper to centre a picture (the whole diagram is one box that flows in
// the text like any other).
\def centerline c {\hbox to:\hsize {\hfil\c\hfil}}

\title{The \texttt{automaton} Package}
\author{A tour of finite-state-machine diagrams in texish}
\maketitle

\section{The vocabulary}

The package adds a handful of commands on top of the \texttt{diagram} layer, each
giving a circle or an arrow its automata-theory meaning. Every machine is declared
inside a \texttt{\\picture}, and the four building blocks are: a \emph{state}, an
\emph{accepting} (final) state, an \emph{initial} marker, and a labelled
\emph{transition}. The figure below shows all four at once.

\vskip 10pt

\centerline{\picture width:4in height:1.4in {
  \state{a}{at 0.7in 0.7in}{$q_0$}
  \accepting{b}{right a 90}{$q_1$}
  \initial{a}
  \trans[$x$]{a}{b}
}}

\vskip 6pt

\noindent Here \texttt{\\state} drew the ordinary circle $q_0$, \texttt{\\accepting}
drew the double circle $q_1$, \texttt{\\initial} attached the short incoming arrow,
and \texttt{\\trans} drew the labelled transition between them.

\section{States}

A state is a circle sized to its label; an accepting state carries a second ring
drawn just inside the outline. The gap between those two rings is configurable with
\texttt{\\autoring}. Below, an ordinary state sits beside an accepting one so the
double ring is easy to compare.

\vskip 10pt

\centerline{\picture width:3.2in height:1.2in {
  \state{s}{at 0.6in 0.55in}{ordinary}
  \accepting{f}{right s 70}{accepting}
}}

\subsection{The initial marker}

\texttt{\\initial} marks the start state with a short arrow entering from the left.
For a start arrow coming from another side, the underlying \texttt{\\dgentry}
primitive stays available: it takes a direction (\texttt{north}, \texttt{south},
\texttt{east} or \texttt{west}) and a length. The three states below are each
marked as a start state, but from different sides.

\vskip 10pt

\centerline{\picture width:4.4in height:1.5in {
  \state{w}{at 0.8in 0.75in}{west}
  \state{n}{right w 70}{north}
  \state{s2}{right n 70}{south}
  \initial{w}
  \dgentry{n}{north}{30}
  \dgentry{s2}{south}{30}
}}

\section{Transitions}

\subsection{Straight transitions}

\texttt{\\trans[symbol]\{from\}\{to\}} draws a straight labelled arrow between two
states. The label is set beside the arrow, and the arrow attaches to the true
circle boundary at each end. A transition on more than one symbol simply lists them
in the label.

\vskip 10pt

\centerline{\picture width:4in height:1.2in {
  \state{p}{at 0.7in 0.6in}{$p$}
  \state{q}{right p 100}{$q$}
  \trans[$a, b$]{p}{q}
}}

\subsection{Self-transitions}

\texttt{\\loopabove\{name\}\{symbol\}} draws a self-transition as a labelled loop
rising from the top of the state — the standard picture for a symbol that keeps the
machine in the same state.

\vskip 10pt

\centerline{\picture width:2.6in height:1.4in {
  \state{r}{at 1.3in 0.5in}{$q$}
  \loopabove{r}{$a, b$}
}}

\subsection{Curved transitions for a back-and-forth pair}

When two states transition to each other, two straight arrows would overlap.
\texttt{\\arc[bend]\{from\}\{to\}\{symbol\}} bows the arrow sideways by \texttt{bend}
points; giving each direction the same bend separates the pair into two clean arcs.

\vskip 10pt

\centerline{\picture width:3in height:1.4in {
  \state{u}{at 0.7in 0.7in}{$0$}
  \state{v}{right u 80}{$1$}
  \arc[28]{u}{v}{$a$}
  \arc[28]{v}{u}{$b$}
}}

\section{A complete DFA}

Putting the pieces together: this deterministic finite automaton accepts strings
over $\{a, b\}$ that contain the substring $ab$. The machine stays in $q_0$ until an
$a$ is read, waits in $q_1$ for the following $b$, and once in the accepting state
$q_2$ it stays there for the rest of the input. Each state carries the self-loops
for the symbols that leave it unchanged.

\vskip 12pt

\centerline{\picture width:5in height:1.7in {
  \state{q0}{at 0.7in 0.7in}{$q_0$}
  \state{q1}{right q0 78}{$q_1$}
  \accepting{q2}{right q1 78}{$q_2$}
  \initial{q0}
  \trans[$a$]{q0}{q1}
  \trans[$b$]{q1}{q2}
  \loopabove{q0}{$b$}
  \loopabove{q1}{$a$}
  \loopabove{q2}{$a, b$}
}}

\section{Layout is free}

States need not sit in a row — any placement the \texttt{diagram} layer understands
works. Absolute coordinates (\texttt{at X Y}) and the relative directions
(\texttt{right}, \texttt{left}, \texttt{above}, \texttt{below}, each with an optional
gap) can be mixed freely. Placed at the corners of a triangle, the three states
below count the number of $1$s read modulo three; each $1$ steps to the next state,
and $r_0$ — both the start and the only accepting state — is reached again exactly
when the count is divisible by three. The transitions are curved arcs bowing around
the cycle.

\vskip 10pt

\centerline{\picture width:3.2in height:2.3in {
  \accepting{m0}{at 1.5in 1.9in}{$r_0$}
  \state{m1}{at 0.6in 0.5in}{$r_1$}
  \state{m2}{at 2.4in 0.5in}{$r_2$}
  \initial{m0}
  \arc[-30]{m0}{m1}{$1$}
  \arc[-30]{m1}{m2}{$1$}
  \arc[-30]{m2}{m0}{$1$}
}}

\section{A nondeterministic automaton}

Nondeterminism needs no new commands: a state may simply have several transitions
on the same symbol. This NFA accepts strings over $\{a, b\}$ that end in $ab$. The
start state $n_0$ loops on every symbol while guessing where the suffix begins,
takes an $a$ to $n_1$, then a $b$ to the accepting state $n_2$.

\vskip 12pt

\centerline{\picture width:5in height:1.7in {
  \state{n0}{at 0.7in 0.7in}{$n_0$}
  \state{n1}{right n0 90}{$n_1$}
  \accepting{n2}{right n1 90}{$n_2$}
  \initial{n0}
  \loopabove{n0}{$a, b$}
  \trans[$a$]{n0}{n1}
  \trans[$b$]{n1}{n2}
}}

\section{Tuning the look}

The configuration of the underlying \texttt{diagram} layer remains in reach, so the
appearance can be tuned without leaving the package. Overriding \texttt{dgfill} (the
state fill), \texttt{dglinecolor} (the ink), \texttt{autoring} (the double-ring gap)
and \texttt{dgloop} (the height of a self-loop) restyles the same machine below. The
overrides are confined to this picture's group, so they do not leak into the rest of
the document.

\vskip 12pt

\centerline{{
  \set dgfill {#fdeedd}
  \set dglinecolor {#7a3b00}
  \set autoring {4.5}
  \set dgloop {32}
  \picture width:4in height:1.7in {
    \state{t0}{at 0.7in 0.7in}{$s_0$}
    \accepting{t1}{right t0 100}{$s_1$}
    \initial{t0}
    \trans[$1$]{t0}{t1}
    \loopabove{t0}{$0$}
    \loopabove{t1}{$0, 1$}
  }
}}

\vskip 10pt

\noindent That completes the tour: states ordinary and accepting, the initial
marker from any side, straight, looping and curved transitions, free placement, and
the styling knobs — all the package has to offer.
